{"name":"Blackberry Farm","hex":"#62506b","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blackberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fruit-bearing shrub of the aggregate species Rubus fruticosus and some hybrids.","The soft fruit borne by this shrub, formed of a black (when ripe) cluster of drupelets."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To gather or forage for blackberries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blackberry"},{"term":"farm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place where agricultural and similar activities take place, especially the growing of crops or the raising of livestock.","A tract of land held on lease for the purpose of cultivation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To work on a farm, especially in the growing and harvesting of crops.","To devote (land) to farming."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/farm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blackberry Farm (books)","blocks":[{"text":"Blackberry Farm is a series of 25 children's literature stories. They were written by Jane Pilgrim, and first published around 1950, with copyright for individual titles dated between 1949 and 1967. The original books were illustrated in colour by F. Stocks May. Published by the Brockhampton Press of Leicester, they originally retailed at One Shilling and Sixpence."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/2d/Blackberry_Farm_book_1_-_Emily_the_Goat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackberry_Farm_(books)","wikidata":"Q4922560"},{"title":"Blackberry Farm (resort)","blocks":[{"text":"Blackberry Farm is a luxury 4,200-acre resort in Walland, Tennessee, bordering the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. It was rated the best small hotel in America by Zagat Survey in 2004. Blackberry Farm is part of the Relais & Châteaux association."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Blackberry_Farm_01.jpeg/330px-Blackberry_Farm_01.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackberry_Farm_(resort)","wikidata":"Q16254910"},{"title":"Blackberry","blocks":[{"text":"A blackberry is an edible fruit produced by many species in the genus Rubus in the family Rosaceae, hybrids among these species within the subgenus Rubus, and hybrids between the subgenera Rubus and Idaeobatus. The taxonomy of blackberries has historically been confused because of hybridization and apomixis so that species have often been grouped together and called species aggregates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Ripe%2C_ripening%2C_and_green_blackberries.jpg/330px-Ripe%2C_ripening%2C_and_green_blackberries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackberry","wikidata":"Q19842373"},{"title":"Farm","blocks":[{"text":"A farm is an area of land that is devoted primarily to agricultural processes with the primary objective of producing food and other crops; it is the basic facility in food production. The name is used for specialized units such as arable farms, vegetable farms, fruit farms, dairy, pig and poultry farms, and land used for the production of natural fiber, biofuel, and other biobased products. It includes ranches, feedlots, orchards, plantations and estates, smallholdings, and hobby farms, and includes the farmhouse and agricultural buildings as well as the land. In modern times, the term has been extended to include such industrial operations as wind farms and fish farms, both of which can operate on land or at sea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Church_Farm%2C_Stockton%2C_Norfolk_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1968544.jpg/330px-Church_Farm%2C_Stockton%2C_Norfolk_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1968544.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.o